    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [x] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [x] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n- [x] I have updated 'mini.nvim' to latest version of the `main` branch\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.snippets\n\n### Neovim version\n\n0.12.x\n\n### Description\n\nHello! I'm a big fan of many mini.* plugins. I was trying to integrate mini.snippets into my config when I noticed some really strange behavior: it doesn't seem gracefully handle creating a snippet, and then choosing to delete it.\n\nhttps://github.com/user-attachments/assets/914a6de5-2940-4cdf-b441-37fd1a52e71d\n\nWhat I would expect to happen in this situation is for the entire session to be ended, I think the empty extmarks appearing on the next line is not good UX (I'm not familiar with neovim plugin development so it took me while to understand what was happening).\n\nFrom reading source code and examples, I've come to these assumptions (please correct me if I'm wrong on any):\n- Currently the plugin seems to be trying to support exiting from insert mode and coming back, resuming the snippet\n- However, it only seems to invalidate the session if the line where a node exists is not present anymore (this only seems to work at the end of the file, as you can see from the example above, I can simply add some text after the snippet and the line still \"exists\", neovim moves extmarks to the \"next\" line based on their gravity)\n\n### Reproduction\n\nThe config to reproduce this is very simple, I created a simple config with the lazy plugin manager and just mini.snippets + friendly-snippets, no custom or esoteric configuration. If this is not sufficient, please let me know:\n\nhttps://codeberg.org/ellnix/repro_minisnippets_remaining_extmarks\n\nHow to reproduce (with the `foreach` php snippet):\n1. Open a php file (or `set ft=php`)\n2. Add 3 of lines (either blank or with text)\n3. At the top of the file, run the `foreach` snippet\n4. Exit to normal mode, delete the snippet\n5. Enter insert mode again\n6. Observe the extmarks and tabslots on the next line",
